{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-163205",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[{x}^{2}+xy]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}([{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}]-[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5{y}^{3}}{12}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[x]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=4.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =f(a,4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 90 & =(2a+4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 10 & =2a+4 \\\\ 6 & =2a \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(3,4)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
